title: Skills That Level Up — Graduated Knowledge Architecture
summary: 'Deep exploration of a 5-level knowledge graduation system (CLAUDE.md → Skills → Protocols → Aspects → Hooks) where knowledge enters expensive and explicit, then graduates to cheaper levels as confidence proves it. Ripple becomes the skill router. Confidence calibration becomes the graduation signal. Core thesis: systems grow in capability without growing in cost when knowledge can be promoted to cheaper enforcement layers.'
symbols_touched:
  - '#paradigm-mcp'
decisions:
  - id: leveling-framework
    decision: Knowledge should graduate through 5 levels (CLAUDE.md → Skills → Protocols → Aspects → Hooks) with confidence as the promotion signal
    rationale: Each level is cheaper to execute. Systems grow in capability without growing in token cost. Confidence calibration already exists — it just needs to become structurally load-bearing.
  - id: derivation-runtime-split
    decision: Paradigm owns skill derivation (generating from structure), Claude Code owns skill runtime (execution)
    rationale: Tight coupling gives symbol-aware routing but risks portability. Owning derivation but not runtime keeps Paradigm as the knowledge graph while letting Claude Code evolve its execution independently.
